you Any further comments All in favor signify by saying I I Seeing that that vote is unanimous the recommendation of the finance committee will be to approve at the next meeting of the governing body Next item number 31F consideration of bid number S-9-26 for a contract between the city of Cheyenne and Ryman Corp for the Story Boulevard extension extension construction project Staff report Mr Mayor Mr Chairman members of the committee for the record I'm Mayor Patrick Collins Before you and for your consideration is a contract with Ryman Corporation to extend Story Boulevard east to Whitney Road The bid amount is not to exceed 3,493,630 and a Scott Royal 85 This is a really important extension for the people living in the east part of our community and

it's designed to take the pressure off an already very busy Del Range Boulevard It's going to come become much more important next summer when the city closes Del Range Boulevard from College Drive to Van Beern to widen and improve Del Range through that road section And the new extension will help take that diverted traffic It was originally planned to be built for reconstruction um of the intersection of uh Del Range and Whitney that was going to be started by WOT later this year But unfortunately the county didn't approve the original design because it had curbon gutter That curbon gutter was there due to a high-pressure gas line that was permitted in the county section and

buried at a depth that would not allow for the traditional ditch along the side of the road So we had to go back and redesign the road by lifting up the road to allow the county designed dradage ditch I'm here to present because the level of county participation is has been in question As a project was being designed we received a letter from the county commissioners agreeing to pay for $727,986.60 which would have been the cost to build that as a county gravel road for their section of the road That was before we received bids or had a good handle on what the actual cost of the road extension would be The traffic study came back saying that on day one and

without any detour traffic this road would hold about 3,500 cars a day and that would exceed the county standard for a a gravel road And then we received a second letter agreeing to increase that participation to $936,6067 and they would pay for half of the cost to pave the county section of the project And um what I understand is they would pay for a two-inch lift of asphalt And I'm assuming they would hope that the city would pay for the remaining um and I don't know that the twoinch lift would have held up to the amount of traffic that was on that road But we finally received the bids and engineer Cobb was able to speak with a member of the county commission directly And

after that conversation we received a third letter uh agreeing to pay $1,161,510.60 And that was their final position on their participation on this road That reflects a $34,393 shortfall on the bid cost to build the county's portion of the road The calculated portion for the county is $1,465,930.82 Now the county has identified a couple components that they don't think they should have to pay for Now when you go to College Drive on the western edge of the project it's much higher than Summit Drive And uh the steep angle is not appropriate for an intersection of this kind especially with the numbers of traffic that's going to be going through it and the speeds The design includes building up the approach reducing the angle to a safe percentage That work would require a retaining wall and

the county commission has said they don't want to pay for that in the amount of $124,920 The design also has some curbing to direct the uh storm water flow off College Drive in the amount of $57,49 And also the approach to college drive needs to be m made out of a material to ask for good friction for acceleration onto college drive and for the cars turning onto summit drive The design calls for a small section of concrete to make sure those call the vehicles can safely make that transition and that cost us $43,400 Those are the major parts of that $300,000 And so here we are today and I think we have three choices Um

we could just not build this project That's an option for us We could reject bids We could do the project and accept the county's offer of the $1161,000 um and have the Cheyenne taxpayers pay the additional $34,930.85 Thank you Scott Or we could build the city's portion of the road and let the county decide to do what to do with their portion of it From my perspective the money's been budgeted and it will never be cheaper to build the extension than it is today And so um I guess Mr uh chairman Mr Cobb is here Uh he can show you the section of the road and uh some of the things we were just speaking about but we do have a quandry before us and

and what what should we do what should we pay for and um so I wanted to come and explain the the challenge before us today and look for your guidance and how we should uh move forward and I'm happy to answer any questions or we can wait till Mr Cobb is finished and then uh we can determine how to move forward from there Okay committee You have any questions for either the engineer cob or the mayor We did a little quick PowerPoint I have a quick question then So do you have an idea of how many feet exactly the county's portion would remain gravel A quarter mile or chairman It's approximately a mile each there roughly I'll show you in a map here in a second just off the top of my head There you go If you could just advance to the second one please chairman Tom City Engineer Just so

you can see what I've identified here is the blue portion to the left of the screen is that we we basically broke this into geographical means So if you recall um about 3 and a half four years ago now um I came in front of some of you that are here now um and we purchased the property on the the the right side of the screen from the Whitney Geel family for approximately $516,000 The purpose of that was to make this connection It's as the mayor has indicated I've said this for a long time this is a vital connection to support emergency services and to be able to let those folks in those rural areas be able to move from from east to west The only two avenues they have on the northeast corner are moving up to Iron Mountain or Del Range Boulevard And

so those two corridors most folks go to Del Range which is part of our issue right now that I've got a lot of constraints and a lot of volume on that road So this connection will help alleviate that in the future And as the mayor indicated um both construction projects would benefit from this being a detour route We do expect you know that like the mayor had indicated over 3,000 cars a day to immediately hit this The minute we make the connection it goes from 84 cars a day on what would be the blue section of the county section of this to to to that 3,000 mark And then I did have the no do a model for blocking or for for both construction projects We modeled it by assuming Del Range and

College closed and Del Range and Van Beern were closed and at various locations that the amount of traffic goes up to 5,200 cars a day on something that used to see 84 cars Um so roughly you can see it's a little less than a mile in the county section or just right at and we're we're at more at the mile So just under a mile for the county a little a mile for ours Does that answer your question sir Um just to show you the next slide please Jennifer I did kind of indicate this is what I got from Commissioner Malm yesterday on the items that the mayor indicated were removed They were highlighted in red and

they don't quite line up The their printing that they furnished back to me I didn't couldn't line it up perfectly but it gives you an idea It's storm sewer curbon gutter um some surfacing that are in that western section Could you go to the next slide please Now showing you on the second page same thing And then the third slide So I tried to highlight those areas This is the far west side in the plan set Um this is the curb and gutter and the the concrete fillets that are at the very edge as tying into College Drive And as the mayor indicated we we did have to to to raise this up in order to make it a much smoother transition and

allows something to be a a lot more stable for when we have traffic that enters into College Drive And again we're going from 84 cars a day to 3,000 cars a day the minute we open it up Um that retaining wall had to be placed It's in highlighted in red on the on the bottom of the page It it goes along the property which we refer to as the stone property Um we we we there was no other way to do it U Mr Stone did not want fill on his property So the only way to do it was to the retaining wall in indicated you can't not do the fill because we need to slopes are around 8 to 13% depending on where you're at and

then we got it down to about uh 5.5 Um the the storm sewer is highlighted in the clouded version It runs across There's a single inlet that dumps into the ditch on the north north end side And then the two driveways um the county also did not want to pay for the paved approaches off the roadway both north and south And the driveway had to be done on the south side because again we're lifting the road up So it's completely modifying the approach into their house Um next slide please This is an extension down the curb and gutter continues down both sides until you get through that area the retaining wall And you can see on the north it goes directly into the ditch In the south they carried it a little bit further to the the next approach And

